28:[{"w":[0],"s":"ok","c":"Start with prompt engineering, then RAG, fine-tuning last","e":"\"Prompt engineering is typically the best place to start.\"","u":["https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/optimizing-llm-accuracy"]},{"w":[0],"s":"ok","c":"Missing facts (knowledge gap) → RAG; wrong behavior/format/consistency → prompting, then fine-tuning","e":"RAG when model lacks context; fine-tuning for \"consistency of behavior\" and formatting","u":["https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/optimizing-llm-accuracy"]},{"w":["k"],"s":"fix","c":"Fine-tuning doesn't fix a knowledge gap, only a behavior gap","e":"FT gives modest knowledge gains; \"RAG consistently outperforms it\" for existing and new knowledge","u":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.05934"]},{"w":[1],"s":"fix","c":"RAG = an embedding pipeline plus a vector store","e":"Production RAG commonly combines embeddings with BM25 lexical search","u":["https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval"]},{"w":[1],"s":"ok","c":"Updating RAG's source is a re-index, not a re-train","e":"RAG supplies context at inference; no model training involved","u":["https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/optimizing-llm-accuracy"]},{"w":[1],"s":"fix","c":"Fine-tuning usually needs thousands of good examples, not hundreds","e":"\"minimum ... is 10\"; \"We see improvements from fine-tuning on 50–100 examples\"","u":["https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/supervised-fine-tuning"]},{"w":[1],"s":"ok","c":"Fine-tuning requires an eval harness; without evals it's blind","e":"\"Only invest in fine-tuning after setting up evals.\"","u":["https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/supervised-fine-tuning"]},{"w":[1],"s":"ok","c":"A fine-tuned model can get worse at tasks it used to handle (regressions)","e":"\"Catastrophic forgetting is generally observed in LLMs ranging from 1b to 7b\"","u":["https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.08747"]},{"w":[1],"s":"ok","c":"Base-model updates force a decision: retrain or stay on the old version","e":"Fine-tuned inference continues \"until the base models are deprecated\"; no new fine-tunes on deprecated bases","u":["https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/deprecations"]},{"w":[1],"s":"fix","c":"RAG doesn't have the base-model-update problem at all","e":"OpenAI shut down first-gen embedding models Jan 4, 2024; replacing embedding model means re-embedding corpus","u":["https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/deprecations"]},{"w":[2],"s":"ok","c":"Distillation moves an expensive model's behavior into a cheaper model","e":"\"Fine-tuning smaller, cost-efficient models using outputs from more capable models\"","u":["https://openai.com/index/api-model-distillation/"]},{"w":[2],"s":"ok","c":"Production usually combines techniques; fine-tuning stacks with RAG rather than replacing it","e":"\"likely you may end up with fine-tuning + RAG in your production solution\"","u":["https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/optimizing-llm-accuracy"]},{"w":["d"],"s":"ok","c":"Lie: fine-tuning is the right first move for missing facts (must be false)","e":"Guide: start with prompting; RAG for missing knowledge — lie is false as intended","u":["https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/optimizing-llm-accuracy"]},{"w":["d"],"s":"fix","c":"Steps: distillation is a separate, cheaper step placed before fine-tuning","e":"Distillation is defined as fine-tuning a smaller model on a larger model's outputs","u":["https://openai.com/index/api-model-distillation/"]}],
